{% comment %}
=====================================================================
 Spent time — the same tag, pointed at the other table
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 Set the template's SOURCE to "Spent time" and its OUTPUT to "One
 document for the whole set". The report then resolves through a
 spent-time query rather than an issue query, and the variable is
 `time_entries` instead of `issues`.

 `{% sql_aggregate %}` sums HOURS here rather than counting issues, and
 that is the default rather than something you ask for: `bucket.count`
 carries the measure, which is hours on this source and a count of issues
 on the other. A template written against one source therefore reads the
 other, which is the point of there being one tag.

 THREE THINGS THIS SOURCE DOES DIFFERENTLY, and each is refused VISIBLY
 rather than ignored — a refusal appears under the preview as a "reduced"
 note naming the reason:

   * `from:` is not used. The scope comes from the template's source and
     the saved query on the page; use `query_id:` to point at a saved
     SPENT-TIME query.
   * `measure:` takes `hours` (the default) or `count`. There is no
     `of:` — the field is decided by the source.
   * there are no drill-through URLs and no custom-field dimensions, and
     no period series either: a time entry is not opened and closed, so a
     `{% sql_aggregate %}` with no `group_by` is refused here.

 The dimensions on the entry itself are `activity`, `user`, `project` and
 `issue`; `tracker`, `status`, `version` and `category` need the entry
 joined to its issue, which a saved spent-time query provides.

 ONE OF THOSE FOUR CANNOT BE NAMED TODAY. A tag parameter that is a bare
 word is resolved as a Liquid variable, and `user` and `project` are
 ALWAYS variables in a report — so `group_by: user` asks for
 `group_by: "Redmine Admin"` and the page says so. Quoting does not help:
 the quotes are stripped before the lookup. It is a defect in the tag
 rather than in this starter, it is reported, and until it is fixed the
 two dimensions below are the ones to use.

 YOU SEE THE HOURS YOUR ROLE LETS YOU SEE. If a role may only read its
 own time entries, the report says so above the numbers rather than
 quietly reporting one person's timesheet as the project's total.
=====================================================================
{% endcomment %}

{% sql_aggregate group_by: activity, assign_to: by_activity %}
{% sql_aggregate group_by: issue, sort: count, limit: 15, assign_to: by_issue %}

<h1>Spent time — {{ project.name }}</h1>
<p class="rrd-muted">{{ by_activity.total }} hours in scope</p>

<div class="rrd-card">
  {% chart id: activity, from: by_activity, title: "Hours by activity", y_title: "Hours" %}
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <h2>By activity</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Activity</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Hours</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for bucket in by_activity.buckets %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ bucket.label }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ bucket.count }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
    <tfoot>
      <tr>
        <th>Total</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">{{ by_activity.total }}</th>
      </tr>
    </tfoot>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <h2>Where the hours went</h2>
  {% comment %} An issue you may not see is named `#42` and nothing else — the hours are yours
     to count, the subject is not yours to read. That is Redmine's own rule in its spent-time
     report and this axis follows it rather than inventing a second one. {% endcomment %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Issue</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Hours</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for bucket in by_issue.buckets %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ bucket.label }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ bucket.count }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
